          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Work, Energy, and Simple Machines"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.017,
            0.952,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 123"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.086,
            0.775,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 7.6:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A jet aircraft of mass 15000 kg lands on the deck of an aircraft carrier (Fig. 7.12). To stop the aircraft within the short length of the deck a hook on the aircraft’s tail is caught in a wire stretched across the deck. The wire exerts an approximately constant backward force of 367500 N and stops the jet within 100 m. What was the velocity of the aircraft just before the wire caught the hook?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.191,
            0.572,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Answer:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let the aircraft approach with a velocity v"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.211,
            0.644,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Initial kinetic energy of the aircraft "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "K = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 15000   kg  \\times \\nu^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Final kinetic energy of the aircraft "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "K   = 0 \\; \\mathrm { J }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.264,
            0.607,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Change in the kinetic energy "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sigma = 0 \\mathrm{J} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 15000    kg  \\times \\nu^2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " From Eq. (7.3),"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.664,
            0.19,
            0.953,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.664,
                0.19,
                0.953,
                0.328
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.311,
            0.654,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Change in the kinetic energy = the work done by the wire. The displacement of the aircraft and the force that the wire applies are in opposite directions. Therefore, the work done by the force applied by the wire will be negative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.678,
            0.334,
            0.943,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 7.12: A jet aircraft landing on an aircraft carrier"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.386,
            0.743,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using Eq. (7.2), the work done by the wire "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "F \\times S = 367500\\  N  \\times (-100\\  m )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Substituting the values in Eq. (7.3), we obtain"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.426,
            0.531,
            0.458
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "-\\frac{1}{2} \\times 15000\\  kg  \\times \\upsilon^{2} = - (367500\\  N  \\times 100\\  m )"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.461,
            0.692,
            0.496
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "v^{2}=\\frac{36750\\times2}{15}\\ \\mathrm{N}\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ \\mathrm{kg}^{-1}=4900\\ \\mathrm{kg}\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ \\mathrm{s}^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ \\mathrm{kg}^{-1}=4900\\ \\mathrm{m}^{2}\\ \\mathrm{s}^{-2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.5,
            0.609,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\nu = 7 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " m "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "s ^ { - 1 } = 2 5 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " km "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { h } ^ { - 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " towards the aircraft carrier."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.528,
            0.389,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.561,
            0.93,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Two objects A and B of mass m and 4 m have the same kinetic energy. What is the ratio of the magnitude of velocities of A and B?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.596,
            0.928,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Does the kinetic energy of an object which moves with constant velocity change with its position?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.639,
            0.355,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.4.2 Potential energy"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.661,
            0.775,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Chapter 6, How Forces Affect Motion, you performed an activity (6.1) where you could move a stack of coins by releasing the stretched rubber band with which it was in contact. Have you ever played with a slingshot (gulel)? In this case, when you release the stretched elastic band, the object in contact with it shoots in the forward direction (Fig. 7.13a). You might have also watched an archery competition. The archer pulls on the string of the bow bending its arms in the process. When released, the bow comes back along with the string to its original position, and the arrow flies off the bow (Fig. 7.13b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.79,
            0.651,
            0.94,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 18,
              "bbox": [
                0.79,
                0.651,
                0.94,
                0.766
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_18.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 19,
              "bbox": [
                0.786,
                0.771,
                0.961,
                0.787
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.13: (a) A slingshot"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.814,
            0.776,
            0.951
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In these examples, you notice that when the stretched band or the bent bow is released, it applies a force on the object in contact with it. This sets the object in motion giving it kinetic energy. At the same time, the stretched band or bent bow comes back to its original shape. The stack of coins, ball and the arrow thus gain the kinetic energy they did not originally possess. This energy must have come from the stretched band and the bent bow, which have the capacity to do work due to their shape. The work done by the force which was applied to cause these deformations was stored in the band or the bow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.817,
            0.968,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 21,
              "bbox": [
                0.782,
                0.817,
                0.968,
                0.913
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_21.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 22,
              "bbox": [
                0.792,
                0.918,
                0.953,
                0.948
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.13: (b) Shooting an arrow using a bow"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
